  "slug": "zero-dna-mirrordna-8a09",
  "summary": "New preprints cut DNA and protein language-model cost via data pruning, learned tokenizers and small backbones, as benchmarks expose homology leakage.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is reducing the cost of biomolecular language models without losing accuracy. Sequence Pruning dynamically drops DNA pretraining data, reducing processing by up to 96%. MirrorDNA learns a tokenizer that enforces reverse-complement symmetry and matches larger models while staying compact. HyPLM-Flash (650M parameters, 726B tokens) matches ESM-2 and ESM C on ProteinGym and contact precision at lower cost. The 1.96M-parameter HyInv inverse-folding network and the MELD mutation-effect model, trained on wild-type versus mutant logit differences, also report gains over larger baselines. The Biophysical Transformer changes the pretraining input itself, using Poisson-Boltzmann electrostatic fields. A second strand questions how these gains are measured: Protify reports task dependence and homology leakage in PLM evaluation, and RPFace introduces a stratified protein-RNA interface benchmark that exposes structural bias. LESS-PseU and TriTower-m6Am apply DNABERT2 and RNA-FM embeddings to RNA modification-site prediction.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-12"
}
